Use a reference triangle with opposite side $7$ and hypotenuse $11$.

$\begin{aligned}\text{Adj}&=\sqrt{11^2-7^2}\\&=\sqrt{72}\\&=6\sqrt{2}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,3.3)--cycle;
\draw (3.75,0)--(3.75,0.25)--(4,0.25);
\path (2,0) node[below] {$6\sqrt{2}$};
\path (4,1.65) node[right] {$7$};
\path (2,1.65) node[above left] {$11$};
\path (0.65,0.25) node {$\theta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An acute angle lies in Q1, where cosine and tangent are positive.

$\begin{aligned}\cos\theta&=\frac{6\sqrt{2}}{11}\\\tan\theta&=\frac{7}{6\sqrt{2}}\end{aligned}$

The reference triangle has adjacent side $5$ and opposite side $2$.

$\begin{aligned}\text{Hyp}&=\sqrt{5^2+2^2}\\&=\sqrt{29}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[->] (-0.4,0)--(4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.3)--(0,0.7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (0,0)--(4,-1.6)--(4,0);
\draw (3.75,0)--(3.75,-0.25)--(4,-0.25);
\path (2,0) node[above] {$5$};
\path (4,-0.8) node[right] {$2$};
\path (2,-0.8) node[below left] {$\sqrt{29}$};
\path (2.6,-2) node {Q4};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In Q4, sine is negative and cosine is positive, so secant is positive.

$\begin{aligned}\sin\theta&=-\frac{2}{\sqrt{29}}\\\cos\theta&=\frac{5}{\sqrt{29}}\\\sec\theta&=\frac{1}{\cos\theta}\\&=\frac{\sqrt{29}}{5}\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}\sin\theta&=\frac{1}{\operatorname{cosec}\theta}\\&=\frac{4}{7}\end{aligned}$

Use opposite side $4$ and hypotenuse $7$.

$\begin{aligned}\text{Adj}&=\sqrt{7^2-4^2}\\&=\sqrt{33}\end{aligned}$

Sine is positive and cosine is negative, so $\theta$ lies in Q2. Cotangent and secant are negative.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[->] (-4.5,0)--(0.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5)--(0,3.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (0,0)--(-4,3)--(-4,0);
\draw (-3.75,0)--(-3.75,0.25)--(-4,0.25);
\path (-2,0) node[below] {$\sqrt{33}$};
\path (-4,1.5) node[left] {$4$};
\path (-2,1.5) node[above right] {$7$};
\path (-2,3.5) node {Q2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}\cot\theta&=-\frac{\sqrt{33}}{4}\\\sec\theta&=-\frac{7}{\sqrt{33}}\end{aligned}$

Choose a reference triangle with opposite side $t$ and adjacent side $1$. Let $\beta$ be the acute reference angle at $(0,0)$; $\alpha$ remains the angle in Q3.

$\begin{aligned}\text{Hyp}&=\sqrt{1^2+t^2}\\&=\sqrt{1+t^2}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(3.6,0)--(3.6,2.6)--cycle;
\draw (3.35,0)--(3.35,0.25)--(3.6,0.25);
\draw (0.65,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=35.84,radius=0.65];
\path (0.9,0.28) node {$\beta$};
\path (1.8,0) node[below] {$1$};
\path (3.6,1.3) node[right] {$t$};
\path (1.8,1.3) node[above left] {$\sqrt{1+t^2}$};
\path (1.8,-0.8) node {Reference triangle; not to scale};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In Q3, sine and cosine are negative, so secant is also negative.

$\begin{aligned}\sin\alpha&=-\frac{t}{\sqrt{1+t^2}}\\\sec\alpha&=-\frac{\sqrt{1+t^2}}{1}\\&=-\sqrt{1+t^2}\end{aligned}$
